{"term":"同志","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"tóngzhì","category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"queer umbrella","concept":"queer_identity","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"subculture","crude":5,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":92,"recognition_pct_peak":92,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Originally the Maoist-era word for 'comrade,' reclaimed since the early 1990s by Chinese-speaking LGBTQ+ communities as an inclusive umbrella term for gay, lesbian, bisexual, and queer people.","english_term":"queer / gay (umbrella)","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Popularized in Hong Kong by the 1989 gay film festival and Wong Kar-wai's cinema; carries warmth and solidarity. Mainland internet censorship sporadically blocks the term because it doubles as the Communist Party honorific."}
{"term":"拉拉","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"lālā","category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"lesbian","concept":"lesbian","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"subculture","crude":10,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":75,"recognition_pct_peak":78,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Community-positive self-identifier for a lesbian woman; phonetically derived from the English-loanword 'les' (lesbian) with a Chinese reduplication suffix.","english_term":"lesbian","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Preferred within mainland Chinese lesbian communities over the clinical 女同性恋. Less pejorative than older slurs. Sometimes stylized 拉子 (lāzi) in Taiwan."}
{"term":"T","lang":"zh","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"butch role","concept":"butch_identity","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"subculture","crude":10,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":55,"recognition_pct_peak":60,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The masculine-presenting partner in a Chinese lesbian relationship; from English 'tomboy,' denoting the butch role in the rigid T/P (butch/femme) binary dominant in Chinese-speaking lesbian culture.","english_term":"butch","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Originated in Taiwan, spread to mainland. The T/P binary is more codified and socially enforced than Western butch/femme; a lesbian is often expected to declare one or the other. 'H' (from 'half') is sometimes used for those between T and P."}
{"term":"P","lang":"zh","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"femme role","concept":"femme_identity","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"subculture","crude":10,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":52,"recognition_pct_peak":57,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The feminine-presenting partner in a Chinese lesbian relationship; from 婆 (pó, wife/woman), denoting the femme role in the T/P binary.","english_term":"femme","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"The etymology from 婆 (wife) reinforces gender complementarity within the binary. P-identified women are expected to present conventionally feminine. Paired always with T in the binary system."}
{"term":"弯的","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"wān de","category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"gay / queer","concept":"homosexual","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":20,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":80,"recognition_pct_peak":80,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'the bent one'; colloquial slang for a gay or queer person, calqued directly from British English slang 'bent' (gay).","english_term":"bent (British slang for gay)","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Antonym of 直的 (straight). Used neutrally or with mild affection within community; can be mildly pejorative from outsiders. The bent/straight metaphor arrived in Chinese via translated English media."}
{"term":"直的","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"zhí de","category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"heterosexual","concept":"heterosexual","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":5,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":82,"recognition_pct_peak":82,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'the straight one'; colloquial term for a heterosexual person, borrowed from English 'straight.'","english_term":"straight","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Calque of English 'straight'; universal across Chinese-speaking online communities. Antonym of 弯的. Completely non-pejorative."}
{"term":"伪娘","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"wèiniáng","category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"cross-dressing / femboy","concept":"cross_dressing","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"subculture","crude":25,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":70,"recognition_pct_peak":75,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'fake girl'; a biological male who presents convincingly and attractively as female through dress, makeup, and mannerism, without necessarily identifying as transgender.","english_term":"femboy / trap (internet slang)","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Originated in Chinese anime/manga fandom; does not imply transgender identity. Often used admiringly in Chinese internet culture. Closer to English 'femboy' (non-derogatory aesthetics) than 'trap' (which carries more predatory framing). Distinct from 变装 (cross-dressing) in implying convincing passing rather than temporary costume."}
{"term":"妖","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"yāo","category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"effeminate gay man","concept":"effeminate_gay_man","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":58,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":70,"recognition_pct_peak":72,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'demon' or 'monster spirit'; used as a slur for effeminate or gender-nonconforming men, especially flamboyant gay men, though partially reclaimed in some communities.","english_term":"fairy / queen (derogatory sense)","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Carries supernatural otherness connotations — the yāo is a supernatural creature of ambiguous, seductive nature, neither fully human nor animal. Some gay and trans communities reclaim it with pride; in conservative usage it is hostile. Also applied to gender-nonconforming trans women."}
{"term":"蕾丝","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"léisī","category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"lesbian (soft)","concept":"lesbian","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"euphemism","crude":8,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":60,"recognition_pct_peak":65,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'lace fabric'; used as a soft, aestheticized euphemism for lesbian or lesbian relationships, evoking delicacy and femininity.","english_term":"lesbian","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Phonetically echoes 'les' (from 'lesbian') while adding the lace imagery that reinforces feminine elegance. Softer and less clinical than 女同 or 拉拉. Used heavily in Chinese online 百合 fandom spaces and among women who prefer a less politicized label."}
{"term":"腐女","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"fǔnǚ","category":"internet_subculture","subcategory":"bl fangirl","concept":"bl_fangirl_identity","is_novel_concept":true,"register":"subculture","crude":15,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":72,"recognition_pct_peak":72,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'rotten girl'; a woman who avidly consumes and creates BL (boys' love) fiction and male-male romantic/sexual content, without necessarily being LGBTQ-identified herself.","english_term":"fujoshi / BL fangirl","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Sinicized from Japanese 腐女子 (fujoshi); the 'rotten' is ironic self-deprecation about the moral dubiousness of enjoying gay male content as a straight woman. 腐女 are not necessarily queer; their object of desire is fictional gay male couples rather than real gay men. The male equivalent is 腐男 (fǔnán)."}
{"term":"百合","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"bǎihé","category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"female-female romance","concept":"lesbian","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"subculture","crude":8,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":68,"recognition_pct_peak":70,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'lily flower'; the Chinese/East Asian term for yuri — female-female romantic or sexual content in fiction — and by extension a soft descriptor for real-life lesbian relationships.","english_term":"yuri / lesbian (in fandom contexts)","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Borrowed from Japanese 百合 (yuri); primarily a fandom genre label but increasingly used as an identity descriptor. The lily symbolizes purity, femininity, and elegance in East Asian culture, giving the term an aestheticized rather than clinical valence. Antonym genre term is 腐/BL (male-male)."}
{"term":"搞基","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"gǎo jī","category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"gay male activity","concept":"msm_behavior","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":62,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":78,"recognition_pct_peak":80,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Crude slang meaning 'to engage in gay (male) sexual activity'; 搞 means 'to do/mess with' and 基 is phonetic shorthand for 'gay.'","english_term":"to do gay stuff / to mess around (gay male context)","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"搞 carries an informal, slightly rough connotation (like 'fool around with'). Used dismissively by outsiders and reclaimed ironically within community. Implies both the identity and the sexual act. Does not typically apply to lesbian activity, which has separate vocabulary."}
{"term":"基","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"jī","category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"gay","concept":"gay","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":25,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":83,"recognition_pct_peak":85,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Phonetic shorthand for 'gay,' used as an adjective or noun for gay men; appears in compounds like 搞基, 基友 (gay buddy), and 腐基 (fan-fictional gay pairing).","english_term":"gay","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"The character 基 (foundation/base) was chosen for its phonetic match to 'gay' without carrying a negative semantic meaning, giving the loanword a relatively neutral to affectionate valence. Primarily male-coded; lesbian identity has separate vocabulary."}
{"term":"假小子","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"jiǎ xiǎozi","category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"tomboy","concept":"butch_identity","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":10,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":78,"recognition_pct_peak":80,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'fake boy'; a tomboy or masculine-presenting girl or woman; broadly applied to gender-nonconforming females and associated with butch lesbians within LGBTQ contexts.","english_term":"tomboy","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Less explicitly LGBTQ-coded than T; can describe any girl who behaves boyishly, with or without queer identity. Carries mild affection or curiosity rather than contempt. Within lesbian contexts, functions as a softer synonym for T."}
{"term":"男人婆","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"nánrén pó","category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"butch pejorative","concept":"butch_lesbian_slur","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":58,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":70,"recognition_pct_peak":75,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'man-granny/man-wife'; a derogatory term for a masculine or aggressive woman, implying gender failure and often lesbian identity.","english_term":"bull dyke / mannish woman","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"婆 (pó) normally means old woman or wife; the compound 男人婆 creates an oxymoron (man-woman) that marks the subject as a failed or deviant gender category. More contemptuous than 假小子; used to mock women who violate femininity norms. Partially distinct from T in that 男人婆 is almost always pejorative when used by outsiders."}
{"term":"0号","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"líng hào","category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"sexual role — bottom","concept":"sexual_bottom","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"subculture","crude":32,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":55,"recognition_pct_peak":60,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'Number 0'; the receptive/bottom role in Chinese gay male sexual culture, equivalent to 'bottom' in English gay vernacular.","english_term":"bottom","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Part of a Chinese gay numeric role system (0 = bottom, 1 = top, 0.5 = versatile). The zero evokes nullity and receptiveness. The system is more common in mainland China; Taiwanese and Hong Kong communities more often use 攻/受 or Western top/bottom terminology. Typically disclosed in dating apps and cruising contexts."}
{"term":"1号","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"yī hào","category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"sexual role — top","concept":"sexual_top","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"subculture","crude":32,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":55,"recognition_pct_peak":60,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'Number 1'; the insertive/top role in Chinese gay male sexual culture, equivalent to 'top' in English gay vernacular.","english_term":"top","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Paired with 0号 in the Chinese gay numeric role system. The 1 signals primacy and active agency. Role disclosure via 0/1 is a normalized ritual in mainland Chinese gay dating; refusing to state one's number can be seen as evasive."}
{"term":"0.5号","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"líng diǎn wǔ hào","category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"sexual role — versatile","concept":"sexual_versatile","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"subculture","crude":28,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":48,"recognition_pct_peak":52,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'Number 0.5'; the versatile role in Chinese gay male sexual culture, indicating willingness to take either the top (1) or bottom (0) role.","english_term":"versatile / vers","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"The arithmetic fraction between 0 and 1 elegantly encodes the middle position. Less commonly volunteered in dating contexts because the binary 0/1 system creates social pressure to commit; 0.5 is sometimes viewed with mild suspicion by men seeking a fixed-role partner."}
{"term":"攻","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"gōng","category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"top / dominant role","concept":"sexual_top","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"subculture","crude":22,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":72,"recognition_pct_peak":75,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"'Attack/offense'; the dominant, penetrating, or active partner in a gay male or BL (boys' love) relationship; absorbed from Japanese anime/manga fandom vocabulary into real gay community usage.","english_term":"top / seme (Japanese BL term)","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"From Japanese 攻め (seme, 'attacker') in BL fiction terminology; Chinese fandom uses the character 攻 with its Mandarin reading (gōng) rather than the Japanese reading. Complementary to 受 (shòu). The martial connotation (攻 = military offense) reinforces the active, dominant framing."}
{"term":"受","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"shòu","category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"bottom / submissive role","concept":"sexual_bottom","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"subculture","crude":22,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":72,"recognition_pct_peak":75,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"'Receive/endure'; the submissive, receptive, or passive partner in a gay male or BL (boys' love) relationship; from Japanese BL fiction terminology.","english_term":"bottom / uke (Japanese BL term)","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"From Japanese 受け (uke, 'receiver') in BL fiction. Chinese fandom uses 受 with its Mandarin reading. Widely used in both fandom and real gay community contexts. The character 受 also means 'to suffer' or 'to endure,' which in some BL fiction is stereotypically foregrounded through the uke's emotional suffering."}
{"term":"CP","lang":"zh","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"internet_subculture","subcategory":"romantic pairing","concept":"fandom_ship_pairing","is_novel_concept":true,"register":"subculture","crude":10,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":80,"recognition_pct_peak":82,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Short for 'couple pairing'; originally a fandom term for a shipped romantic/sexual pairing of characters or celebrities, now used broadly for any real-life or fictional couple, especially in LGBTQ and dating discourse.","english_term":"ship / OTP (fandom) or couple (general)","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Derived from English 'couple pairing' but the compound usage is a Chinese internet adaptation with no direct English equivalent. 'We are CP' (我们是CP) means 'we're a couple.' In celebrity and idol culture, fan communities officially designate their favorite pairs as CPs. Pervasive in mainland Chinese social media, Weibo, and BL/idol fandoms."}
{"term":"掰弯","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"bāi wān","category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"orientation conversion","concept":"sexual_orientation_conversion","is_novel_concept":true,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":20,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":68,"recognition_pct_peak":70,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'to pry/bend straight'; to turn a straight person gay, or less commonly to reverse that direction; treats sexual orientation as a pliable rod that can be bent from its default straight position.","english_term":"to turn (someone) gay","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"The physical metaphor of bending something rigid into a curve is vivid and distinctly Chinese — English has no single-word equivalent. Used both jokingly within LGBTQ communities ('I've been bent by this character') and accusatorially by conservative outsiders claiming gay people recruit. The directionality is usually straight→gay; reverse use is rarer."}
{"term":"操你妈","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"cào nǐ mā","category":"insults","subcategory":"maternal sexual insult","concept":"motherfucker_insult","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":98,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":95,"recognition_pct_peak":95,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'fuck your mother'; the most severe maternal-sexual expletive in Mandarin, directed at the listener as an extreme insult or exclamation of rage.","english_term":"motherfucker / fuck your mother","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Cruder and more targeted than 他妈的 because 操 (cào, fuck) explicitly names the sexual act and 你 (nǐ, your, second-person) directly addresses the listener's mother. Among the strongest Mandarin expletives. Strictly censored in all Chinese mainstream media. In extreme anger, may be further intensified with 祖宗 (ancestors) modifiers."}
{"term":"他妈的","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"tā mā de","category":"insults","subcategory":"maternal expletive","concept":"motherfucker_insult","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":65,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":98,"recognition_pct_peak":98,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'his/that mother's [thing]'; a highly common Mandarin expletive expressing frustration, anger, or emphasis; the workhorse general profanity of spoken Mandarin.","english_term":"damn it / fucking hell","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Third-person 他 (his/that) makes it slightly less directly confrontational than 操你妈, which addresses the listener in second person. The literal sexual meaning (fucking his mother) is largely semantically bleached in casual use — it functions as a general-purpose intensifier comparable to English 'fucking' or 'bloody.' Appears across regional dialects and social classes."}
{"term":"妈的","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"mā de","category":"insults","subcategory":"maternal expletive (mild)","concept":"motherfucker_insult","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":48,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":97,"recognition_pct_peak":97,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Shortened form of 他妈的; a mild-to-moderate expletive meaning approximately 'damn' or 'shit'; one of the most ubiquitous Mandarin swear words across all registers.","english_term":"damn / crap / hell","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"The most socially tolerated of the maternal-insult cluster; its sexual etymology (mother-fucking) is almost entirely bleached, functioning as a catch-all intensifier comparable to 'bloody' in British English or 'merde' in French. Appears in Chinese film and literature with minimal censorship compared to its longer forms. Often the first profanity learned by Mandarin language students."}
